  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find $\\text{LCM}(14,18,63).$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $14.$ We easily deduce that $14=2\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $18:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $63:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/936a74a3795c2a8ace97dfddd7d4f2f359acf8968ea4404c2711abb96d6a708d.png)\n\nTherefore, $63={3}^{2}\\cdot 7.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =2\\cdot 7 \\\\ 18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 63 & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $14$, $18$  and $63$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(14,18,63)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7=126.\n$$",
